# Line-ending policy for this repo.
#
# Without this file, behaviour depends on each contributor's core.autocrlf
# setting, so a Windows checkout gets CRLF working files while CI (Linux) and
# every Docker build see LF. That split is not cosmetic here:
#   - frontend/home lints with Prettier, whose endOfLine defaults to "lf", so
#     a CRLF checkout fails `bun run lint` with a "Delete CR" error on every
#     single line of every file.
#   - shell scripts (setup.sh, scripts/*.sh) and every Dockerfile RUN step
#     break outright if they reach a Linux container with CRLF.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` pins BOTH sides: LF in the repository, and LF in the
# working tree on every OS. Git stops rewriting endings on checkout, which is
# what the "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warnings were reporting. Windows
# tooling used here (VS Code, Node, MSVC, PowerShell) all read LF fine.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows shells are the one exception: cmd.exe requires CRLF in batch files.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Patch files are applied byte-for-byte by patch-package; never rewrite them.
*.patch -text
*.diff -text

# Binary assets: never diff, never convert endings.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.wasm  binary
*.tflite binary
*.zip   binary
*.xpi   binary
*.jar   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.eot   binary
*.mp4   binary
*.mp3   binary
*.keystore binary
*.jks   binary

# Lockfiles are generated: keep them out of diffs and language stats, but
# still tracked and still text (LF) so they stay mergeable-looking.
package-lock.json linguist-generated=true
bun.lock          linguist-generated=true
Cargo.lock        linguist-generated=true
